We have enhanced our `t_operand` structure as follows:

typedef struct s_env    t_env;

typedef struct  s_op_token
{
	char        *cnt;
	t_quote_int quotes[MAX_QUOTES_NUM];

}   t_op_token;

typedef struct s_operand
{           
	char        *name; // Path to program with its arguments, redirections and etc.

	int         read_end;               // stdin
	int         write_end;              // stdout

	t_redir     redirs[MAX_REDIRS_NUM]; // Redirections of this operand-program
	size_t      red_cnt;                // Redirections counter

	t_quote_int quotes[MAX_QUOTES_NUM]; // All quotes pairs found in `name`
	size_t      qpair_cnt;              // Counter of quote pairs

	char        **argv;
	int         argc;

	t_op_token  *tokens;                // Tokens of this operand-program
	size_t      token_cnt;

	t_env       vars;					// Variables we created parsing this operand-program
	bool        f_per_cmd;              // Per-command flag for our variables

	pid_t       pid;

}   t_operand;

Each operand is gonna have its own array of tokens and environment variables that will be
copied into common array of environment variables.
We did preliminary redirections parsing where we just read heredocs and copied all other
redirections we found in `name`. We just copied all redirection paths as they are without
expanding anything. Later we'll be updating redirection paths if necessary. Also on this 
preliminary parsing stage we removed all syntax quotes in all heredoc delimiters and if
there were at least one quote pair in the delimiter we lowered `f_expand_body` flag (by
default is was set as true).

Now the updated algorithm.


1. Let's divide operand's string `name` on tokens by spaces
   (which are located outside any quote intervals of course).
   During initialization we store on heap in `cnt` token's content;

2. Update quote intervals for each token

3. LOOP traversing all these tokens;

    3.1. IF token is an assignment (we found = outside any quotes, on the left
		 from = there are some symbols and we haven't found any argument yet);

	    3.1.1. Check LHS (variable's name) for correctness;

		3.1.2. IF variable's name is NOT correct;
			(We're gonna treat this token as an argument)
		    3.1.2.1. GOTO 3.3

		(We're gonna treat this token as an assignment)
		3.1.3. state = IND_QNONE

		3.1.4. LOOP through token symbols

			3.1.4.1. IF current symbol is a double quote
				3.1.4.1.1. IF state is IND_QNONE
					3.1.4.1.1.1. state = IND_QDOUBLE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.1.4.1.1.2. Continue
				3.1.4.1.2. IF state is IND_QDOUBLE
					3.1.4.1.2.1. state = IND_QNONE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.1.4.1.2.2. Continue
				3.1.4.1.3. IF state is IND_QSINGLE
					3.1.4.1.3.1. Copy this quote (cause it's just a regular symbol)
					3.1.4.1.3.2. Mark the corresponding index in qmask[] as IND_QSINGLE
					3.1.4.1.3.3. Continue

			3.1.4.2. IF current symbol is a single quote
				3.1.4.2.1. IF state is IND_QNONE
					3.1.4.2.1.1. state = IND_QSINGLE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.1.4.2.1.2. Continue
				3.1.4.2.2. IF state is IND_QSINGLE
					3.1.4.2.2.1. state = IND_QNONE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.1.4.2.2.2. Continue
				3.1.4.2.3. IF state is IND_QDOUBLE
					3.1.4.2.3.1. Copy this quote (cause it's just a regular symbol)
					3.1.4.2.3.2. Mark the corresponding index in qmask[] as IND_QDOUBLE
					3.1.4.2.3.3. Continue

			3.1.4.3. IF current symbol is ~ that goes immediately after = AND state is IND_QNONE AND the next symbol is / (outside any qutoes) OR
					 IF current symbol is ~ that goes immediately after = AND state is IND_QNONE and then token string terminates
				3.1.4.3.1. Expand ~ by copying its value into the new array
				3.1.4.3.2. Assign the corresponding indices in qmask[] the current `state` value

			3.1.4.4. IF current symbol is $ AND state is NOT IND_QSINGLE
				3.1.4.4.1. Expand variable by copying its value into the new array
				3.1.4.4.2. Assign the corresponding indices in qmask[] the current `state` value

			3.1.4.5. IF current symbol is just a regular symbol
				3.1.4.5.1. Copy it into the new array
				3.1.4.5.2. Assign the corresponding qmask[] index the current `state` value

		3.1.5. END of token symbols LOOP;

		3.1.6. Create a local environment variable and add it into the array of variables of this operand `vars`

	3.2. ELSE IF token is <, <<, > or >> ;
	    3.2.1. Just skip it;
	
	3.3. ELSE (Means current token is a regular argument or a redirection operand);
	    3.3.1. IF the previous token was << (means current token is a heredoc delimiter)
		    3.3.1.1. Just skip it (cause we have already processed it on preliminary parsing stage);

		3.3.2. state = IND_QNONE

		3.3.3. LOOP through token symbols

			3.3.3.1. IF current symbol is a double quote
				3.3.3.1.1. IF state is IND_QNONE
					3.3.3.1.1.1. state = IND_QDOUBLE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.3.3.1.1.2. Continue
				3.3.3.1.2. IF state is IND_QDOUBLE
					3.3.3.1.2.1. state = IND_QNONE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.3.3.1.2.2. Continue
				3.3.3.1.3. IF state is IND_QSINGLE
					3.3.3.1.3.1. Copy this quote (cause it's just a regular symbol)
					3.3.3.1.3.2. Mark the corresponding index in qmask[] as IND_QSINGLE
					3.3.3.1.3.3. Continue

			3.3.3.2. IF current symbol is a single quote
				3.3.3.2.1. IF state is IND_QNONE
					3.3.3.2.1.1. state = IND_QSINGLE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.3.3.2.1.2. Continue
				3.3.3.2.2. IF state is IND_QSINGLE
					3.3.3.2.2.1. state = IND_QNONE (We do NOT copy anything)
					3.3.3.2.2.2. Continue
				3.3.3.2.3. IF state is IND_QDOUBLE
					3.3.3.2.3.1. Copy this quote (cause it's just a regular symbol)
					3.3.3.2.3.2. Mark the corresponding index in qmask[] as IND_QDOUBLE
					3.3.3.2.3.3. Continue

			3.3.3.3. IF current symbol is ~ that it's the only one in entire token AND state is IND_QNONE OR
					 IF current symbol is ~ and it's the frist symbol in token AND state is IND_QNONE AND the next symbol is / (outside any quotes)
				3.3.3.3.1. Expand ~ by copying its value into the new array
				3.3.3.3.2. Assign the corresponding indices in qmask[] the current `state` value

			3.3.3.4. IF current symbol is $ AND state is NOT IND_QSIGNLE
				3.3.3.4.1. Expand variable by copying its value into the new array
				3.3.3.4.2. Assign the corresponding indices in qmask[] the current `state` value

			3.3.3.5. IF current symbol is just a regular symbol
				3.3.3.5.1. Copy it into the new array
				3.3.3.5.2. Assign the corresponding qmask[] index the current `state` value

		3.3.4. END of token symbols LOOP;

		3.3.5. LOOP through all new token's array symbols (it's length should be equal to qmask[]);
			3.3.5.1. IF a * was found on the index which is marked as IND_QNONE in qmask[];
			(means the entire token is globbing mask)
				3.3.5.1.1. Unite all consequtive * in one
				3.3.5.1.2. Expand this wildcard pattern passing the mask into expand_wildcards();
				3.3.5.1.2. Break;
		3.3.6. END of new token's array symbols LOOP;

		3.3.7. IF the previous token was <, >>, or > (means we were handling redirection
			   operand; we don't need to add heredoc here cause we have already processed
			   it on the preliminary redirections pareser stage)
			3.3.7.1. IF globbing gave us more than one coincidence
				3.3.7.1.1. Error "ambiguous redirect"
			3.3.7.2. Update the corresponding redirection's path
		3.3.8. ELSE (means we were handling an argument)
			3.3.8.1. Add this processed token into array of arguments `argv`;
			3.3.8.2. Mark that all variables created while parsing the current operand
					 were per-command variables (basically by setting `f_per_cmd`). This
					 implies we will not be creating any more variables while parsing
					 the current operand. All further operand-tokens will be considered
					 as arguments, not as assignments;

4. END of token LOOP;
